CAT = "mlsChemGen"; SEG = "CHG"
Q = [
('Sodium fluoride preserves blood glucose because it inhibits:',
 'Enolase in glycolysis', ['Hexokinase in red cells', 'Glucose oxidase in reagents', 'Insulin release by cells'],
 'Fluoride blocks enolase, stopping glycolysis by blood cells. It acts slowly, so glucose still falls for the first hour or so unless the sample is chilled or acidified.'),
('Fasting hypoglycemia with high insulin and a low C-peptide suggests:',
 'Injected insulin', ['Insulinoma', 'Sulfonylurea use', 'Adrenal insufficiency'],
 "Pharmaceutical insulin has no C-peptide and suppresses the patient's own release. An insulinoma or a sulfonylurea raises insulin and C-peptide together."),
('Antibodies to GAD65 in a newly diagnosed diabetic adult suggest:',
 'Autoimmune (type 1) diabetes', ['Type 2 diabetes', 'Monogenic MODY', 'Gestational diabetes'],
 'Islet autoantibodies such as GAD65, IA-2 and insulin antibodies mark autoimmune beta cell destruction, including latent autoimmune diabetes in adults. MODY and type 2 diabetes lack them.'),
('Which lipoprotein carries most of the triglyceride made by the liver?',
 'VLDL', ['Chylomicrons', 'HDL', 'LDL'],
 'The liver packages endogenous triglyceride into VLDL, which lipoprotein lipase turns into IDL and LDL. Chylomicrons carry dietary triglyceride from the gut.'),
('Type III hyperlipoproteinemia with a broad beta band is linked to which genotype?',
 'Apo E2/E2', ['Apo E4/E4', 'Apo E3/E3', 'Apo B-48 loss'],
 'Apo E2 binds remnant receptors poorly, so chylomicron and VLDL remnants build up as a broad beta band. Apo E4 is linked with Alzheimer disease risk.'),
('Lipoprotein lipase needs which apolipoprotein as its activator?',
 'Apo C-II', ['Apo A-I', 'Apo B-100', 'Apo C-III'],
 'Apo C-II on chylomicrons and VLDL activates lipoprotein lipase on capillary walls. Apo C-III inhibits it, and apo A-I activates LCAT.'),
('Which lipid result changes most if the patient has not fasted?',
 'Triglycerides', ['HDL cholesterol', 'Total cholesterol', 'Apolipoprotein B'],
 'Chylomicrons from a recent meal raise triglycerides for hours, while total cholesterol and HDL change little. A 9 to 12 hour fast is used when triglycerides must be exact.'),
('Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with a darkly pigmented liver and normal liver enzymes suggests:',
 'Dubin-Johnson syndrome', ['Rotor syndrome', 'Gilbert syndrome', 'Crigler-Najjar type I'],
 'Dubin-Johnson is a defect in canalicular bilirubin excretion with a black liver pigment. Rotor syndrome is similar without pigment; Gilbert and Crigler-Najjar raise unconjugated bilirubin.'),
('Delta bilirubin is conjugated bilirubin covalently bound to:',
 'Albumin', ['Glucuronic acid', 'Haptoglobin', 'Transferrin'],
 'It forms in prolonged cholestasis and lasts as long as albumin, about 3 weeks, which is why direct bilirubin falls slowly after the blockage clears. Glucuronic acid is the conjugating sugar itself.'),
('Attacks of abdominal pain with a high urine porphobilinogen suggest:',
 'Acute intermittent porphyria', ['Porphyria cutanea tarda', 'Erythropoietic protoporphyria', 'Congenital erythropoietic porphyria'],
 'A defect in porphobilinogen deaminase causes PBG and ALA to build up during attacks. Porphyria cutanea tarda causes blistering skin, not abdominal attacks, and PBG is normal.'),
('B-type natriuretic peptide is released mainly from the:',
 'Cardiac ventricles', ['Cardiac atria', 'Renal juxtaglomerular cells', 'Adrenal cortex'],
 'Ventricular muscle releases BNP and NT-proBNP when stretched by volume or pressure, so they help assess heart failure. Atrial natriuretic peptide comes from the atria.'),
('An LD-1 that is higher than LD-2 with a normal troponin most likely reflects:',
 'In vivo hemolysis', ['Liver congestion', 'Skeletal muscle injury', 'Pulmonary infarction'],
 'Red cells are rich in LD-1, so hemolysis or megaloblastic anemia flips the LD-1/LD-2 ratio. A normal troponin argues against heart damage; liver and muscle raise LD-5.'),
('Which alkaline phosphatase is most heat stable at 56 C?',
 'Placental ALP', ['Bone ALP', 'Liver ALP', 'Intestinal ALP'],
 'Placental ALP survives heating at 56 C for 10 minutes; bone ALP is the most heat labile. Heat inactivation is a rough way to suggest the source of a raised ALP.'),
('A raised serum angiotensin-converting enzyme supports a diagnosis of:',
 'Sarcoidosis', ['Primary hyperaldosteronism', 'Acute pancreatitis', 'Renal artery stenosis'],
 'Granulomas of sarcoidosis make ACE, and levels help follow the disease. ACE in the renin system is not used to diagnose aldosterone or renovascular disorders.'),
('High serum amylase, low urine amylase and a normal lipase suggest:',
 'Macroamylasemia', ['Acute pancreatitis', 'Mumps parotitis', 'Renal failure'],
 'Amylase bound to immunoglobulin is too large for glomerular filtration, so it stays in serum. Renal failure also raises serum amylase but does not lower urine amylase this way.'),
('Which protein is the most sensitive short-term marker of protein nutrition?',
 'Transthyretin', ['Albumin', 'Transferrin', 'Ceruloplasmin'],
 "Transthyretin (prealbumin) has a half-life of about 2 days lets it respond within days of a change in intake. Albumin's half-life of about 3 weeks makes it slow to change."),
('Which test identifies the heavy and light chain type of a serum M-protein?',
 'Immunofixation electrophoresis', ['Serum protein electrophoresis', 'Nephelometric IgG level', 'Free light chain ratio'],
 'Immunofixation overlays antisera to each heavy and light chain on the separated proteins. Protein electrophoresis shows the band, and the free light chain ratio shows clonality without the heavy chain.'),
('For a patient on rasburicase, a uric acid sample must be:',
 'Chilled on ice at once', ['Kept at room temperature', 'Drawn into a fluoride tube', 'Frozen before transport'],
 'Rasburicase keeps breaking down uric acid in the tube at room temperature, giving falsely low results. Prechilled tubes, ice and prompt testing stop it.'),
('Acetoacetate in diabetic ketoacidosis can falsely raise creatinine measured by:',
 'Jaffe alkaline picrate method', ['Enzymatic creatininase method', 'IDMS reference method', 'Cystatin C immunoassay'],
 'Acetoacetate and some cephalosporins form colored products with alkaline picrate. Enzymatic methods avoid this interference, and cystatin C is a different analyte.'),
('A raised AFP with a raised hCG in a young man with a testicular mass points to:',
 'Nonseminomatous germ cell tumor', ['Pure seminoma', 'Metastatic hepatocellular carcinoma', 'Prostate carcinoma'],
 'Yolk sac elements make AFP, so a raised AFP rules out pure seminoma, which may raise hCG alone. Hepatocellular carcinoma raises AFP without a testicular mass.'),
]
